is a young nepali artist based in  kathmandu her work is part of a show by  five artists entitled poems from home  that explores ideas of identity culture  and space the exhibition is part of the  kathmandu trianali 2077  nepal's biggest arts festival that runs  throughout the month of march  i like to explore how the mobile phone  and digital technology impact  traditional cultures and our daily lives  which then create a new culture  [Music]  this fourth edition kicked off in  kathmandu on the first of the month  after being postponed several times due  to the pandemic  spread across three historic areas in  the city and five exhibition venues it  features 300 works by local and  international artists  it includes diverse art forms like  multimedia photography paintings and  installations and offers guided tours  and online viewing  shirari badritsarya is a festival  organizer she says her team intends to  show how the art community in nepal has  grown  the curators of this edition  have worked  is about  trying to weave very complex narratives  about issues around gender or queer  bodies or you know indigenous  expressions and in mapping and gardens  and what are these narratives around the  way we see colonization work and how we  see power work and how do we  sort of dismantle some of the ways we  perceive these things  through artists works it's very powerful  the works are in contrast to the  mountain landscapes and religious  paintings visitors often associate with  nepal chirin sherpa used to create  tibetan buddhist paintings called tankas  today he gives the art form a  contemporary take one of few nepali  artists to feature in prestigious  international platforms like the venice  biennale sherpa looks forward to the  kathmandu trinali  it's a wonderful experience and let's  see  let's hope that we can  introduce nepal in a different light  than what we have been understood so far  through our works we're at the paragon  museum a venue for the kathmandu trinali  the works displayed here celebrate the  diversity and explore the connections  between local and global communities  while they reject the exoticization of  the himalaya as a mythical utopia as  nepal's vibrant art community grows  artists say it's important to continue  conversations between traditional and  contemporary art forms to present a  realistic portrait of nepal today  ramyatha limbu al-jazeera kathmandu
 

